  "worked_solution": "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,\u03d5)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=\u27e8x,\\,y,\\,z\u27e9=\u27e8\\rho \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5\u27e9.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho =\u03d5{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,\u03d5) & =\u27e8\u03d5{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,\u03d5{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,\u03d5{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\u03d5{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,\u03d5{sin}^{3}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5,\\,\u03d5{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $\u03d5{sin}^{3}\u2061\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5.$",
